<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">French India</h1><p class="st-meta">1664 CE – 1954 CE · capital Pondicherry</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">France's scattered settlements in India, centred on Pondicherry, which under Dupleix briefly challenged Britain for mastery of the south.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-east-india-company">French East India Company</a> was founded in 1664. It set up a factory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/surat">Surat</a> in 1668, and in 1674 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francois-martin-governor-of-pondicherry">Francois Martin</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pondicherry">Pondicherry</a> on land granted by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/bijapur-sultanate">Bijapur Sultanate</a>. Further settlements followed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandannagar">Chandernagore</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahe-india">Mahe</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malabar-coast">Malabar</a> coast, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karaikal">Karaikal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yanam">Yanam</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pondicherry">Pondicherry</a> became a planned town with a European quarter and Tamil quarters, ruled by a governor. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-francois-dupleix">Joseph-Francois Dupleix</a>, governor from 1742, intervened in the succession disputes of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/carnatic-sultanate">Carnatic Sultanate</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad">Hyderabad</a>, raising Indian troops and briefly dominating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-clive">Robert Clive's</a> victories reversed French fortunes. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1763">Treaty of Paris</a> in 1763 restricted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> to unfortified trading posts.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The settlements were repeatedly occupied by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> and restored after 1815. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandannagar">Chandernagore</a> joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> after a 1949 plebiscite, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pondicherry">Pondicherry</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karaikal">Karaikal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahe-india">Mahe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yanam">Yanam</a> were transferred de facto in 1954, legally in 1962. They form the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puducherry-union-territory">Union Territory of Puducherry</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/french-india.jpg" alt="French India" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Sodacan · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Royal_Standard_of_the_King_of_France.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1664 CE – 1954 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Pondicherry</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Francois Martin, Joseph-Francois Dupleix</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism, Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French, Tamil</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>India</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Etablissements francais dans l'Inde, French establishments in India, Pondichery</dd></dl></aside></div>
